  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.02 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.5 &  & P(D|{F}_{2})=0.03 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.25 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.25+0.5+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $1%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen defective light bulb is of type A, which is represented as $P(A|D).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.01)(0.4)}{(0.02)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.5)+(0.01)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0225} \\\\  & ≈0.178,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
